@meta
id: 10-03
title: The whole chunk, punctuation and all
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 20
minutes: 4
vscode: double-click gets you half of it, so you drag instead
open: package.json

@brief
`iw` stops at punctuation. That is deliberate and usually helpful, but it
makes a mess of anything built out of punctuation — a command line flag,
a kebab-case class name, a dotted path.

Capital `W` is the same pair of objects measured differently: `iW` and
`aW` run from whitespace to whitespace. Whatever is stuck to the thing
comes with it.

`--detectOpenHandles` went into the test scripts while somebody was
chasing a hanging socket in a pull request last month. It makes CI about
forty seconds slower and the leak is fixed. Take it out of both scripts.

Leave the other flags, and leave the JSON valid — no stray double spaces,
no orphaned dashes.

@keys
| `iW` `aW` | inner and around WORD — whitespace to whitespace |
| `daW` | delete the whole chunk and its trailing space |
| `iw` `aw` | the small kind, which stops at punctuation |

@hints
The flag is one thing to you, but `aw` sees `--` and `detectOpenHandles`
as two different words.
--
Capital letters make the big version: `aW` is everything from one space
to the next.
--
Land anywhere in the flag — `fO` will do it — and `daW` removes the
leading dashes, the name and the space after it in one go.

@solution
    5j fO daW
    j fO daW
    :wq<Enter>

Seventeen keystrokes to make two edits that VS Code cannot do with a
double-click at all: double-clicking `detectOpenHandles` leaves the `--`
behind, so you end up dragging, and dragging is where the off-by-one
character comes from.

The distinction is worth saying out loud once: `w` is a programmer's word
and `W` is a typist's word. `order.customerId` is three `w` words and one
`W` word. `'order-shipped'` is five `w` words and one `W` word. When the
punctuation is part of the meaning, use the capital.

Watch out for the greedy edge of `aW`: it takes everything up to the next
space, including a trailing comma, quote or semicolon. That is why the
flag you removed sat in the middle of each script and not at the end.

@start package.json
{
  "name": "@thrive/orders",
  "version": "2.4.0",
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc --project tsconfig.build.json",
    "test": "jest --runInBand --detectOpenHandles --coverage",
    "test:watch": "jest --runInBand --detectOpenHandles --watch",
    "lint": "eslint src --ext .ts --max-warnings 0"
  }
}

@expected package.json
{
  "name": "@thrive/orders",
  "version": "2.4.0",
  "scripts": {
    "build": "tsc --project tsconfig.build.json",
    "test": "jest --runInBand --coverage",
    "test:watch": "jest --runInBand --watch",
    "lint": "eslint src --ext .ts --max-warnings 0"
  }
}
